{"acceptance_length": 6.473684210526316, "acceptance_lengths": [10, 3, 2, 14, 7, 11, 6, 16, 6, 3, 6, 15, 5, 7, 4, 2, 5, 1, 3, 5, 11, 4, 2, 1, 5, 9, 1, 1, 1, 11, 11, 16, 16, 14, 2, 3, 2, 5], "accepted_draft_lengths": [9, 2, 1, 13, 6, 10, 5, 15, 5, 2, 5, 14, 4, 6, 3, 1, 4, 0, 2, 4, 10, 3, 1, 0, 4, 8, 0, 0, 0, 10, 10, 15, 15, 13, 1, 2, 1, 4], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 23421977088, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 23421971456, "benchmark": "mbpp", "completion": "Here's a Python function that checks whether **every odd index** in a list contains an **odd number**:\n\n```python\ndef odd_position(lst):\n    for i in range(1, len(lst), 2):  # iterate over odd indices (1, 3, 5, ...)\n        if lst[i] % 2 == 0:  # if the number at an odd index is even\n            return False\n    return True\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- We loop through the list starting from index `1` (the first odd index), and step by `2` to only check odd indices.\n- For each of these indices, we check if the number is **even**.\n- If any number at an odd index is even, we return `False`.\n- If all odd indices contain odd numbers, we return `True`.\n\n### Test Cases:\n```python\nassert odd_position([2,1,4,3,6,7,6,3]) == True\nassert odd_position([4,1,2]) == True\nassert odd_position([1,2,3]) == False\n```\n\nAll of these assertions will pass with the above function.", "decode_seconds": 1.3871559407562017, "draft_calls": 38, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 116, "mapper_checkpoint_sha256": "ece1269540becf19b73b75b0ca48a5dfd6178ffe372c6606870efbbab8ae580a", "method": "relay_base", "output_hash": "e1336780ded723ff29391460891ebc1202d4f3d0a80248875ffbafaf35e3c132", "output_tokens": 245, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 23546542592, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 23808966656, "problem_id": "Mbpp/775", "profile_regions_ms": {"draft": 237.8509120941162, "prefill_full_target": 32.880863189697266, "prefill_relay": 0.3412480056285858, "relay": 12.477280050516129, "unattributed_runtime": 15.46968444809317